    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Zones of intrastratal en echelon fractures are prevalent throughout the siliceous and diatomaceous shales of the Monterey Formation, California. The formation of these zones previously has been attributed to fluidized sediments escaping from underlying sedimentary layers. Based on the fractures' geometrical characteristics, lithology controlled propagation and a knowledge of the structural processes that have occurred in the Monterey Formation, this study proposes an alternative formation mechanism. We believe that bed-parallel shear is the driving stress for the creation of the zones of en echelon fractures. Initial studies also indicate that these are the features that primarily control the permeability structure of such zones.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
